<http://data.silknow.org/event/40b6dd58-7abf-3296-962b-7d9d44d9cfd4>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "The vendor of this dress, which was purchased at auction in 1983, stated that this dress had been a wedding dress from 1869, but no further details are known.\n\nIt was acquired as an example of Princess line construction, made popular by the couturier Charles Frederick Worth in the 1860s. The \"Princess Line\" was essentially a fitted dress made using long shaped panels to avoid a horizontal waist seam which separated the bodice from the skirt. Early examples of this style are rare."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/33a8eb4f-6352-34be-bc23-cac86593941b> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The vendor of this dress, which was purchased at auction in 1983, stated that this dress had been a wedding dress from 1869, but no further details are known.\n\nIt was acquired as an example of Princess line construction, made popular by the couturier Charles Frederick Worth in the 1860s. The \"Princess Line\" was essentially a fitted dress made using long shaped panels to avoid a horizontal waist seam which separated the bodice from the skirt. Early examples of this style are rare."@en .

